# udev/90-dock-lid-holdoff.rules
# ==============================
#
# Keeps a lid closed around a USB-C plug-in from acting before logind can see
# the dock. logind/lid-locks-never-suspends.conf makes a lid-close lock the
# screen, so without this, closing the lid as you dock locks the screen you are
# about to use. (Before 2026-10-29 the lid suspended, and on 2026-10-19 a
# suspend into a half-enumerated dock hung the laptop until a forced power-off.)
#
# Every USB-C partner add starts one instance of dock-lid-holdoff@.service,
# which blocks lid handling for 30 s. The partner device is the EARLIEST signal
# a plug-in gives: measured 1.8 s before logind reports external power and 3 s
# before the dock's Thunderbolt link. The instance is named
# <kernel name>-<SEQNUM>, so every plug-in gets a fresh window. If SEQNUM were
# ever missing the name is still valid ("port1-partner-") and only the
# per-plug-in freshness is lost. `add` ONLY: partners also get `change`
# uevents, and matching those would start a fresh 30 s instance on every one of
# them.
#
# SOURCE OF TRUTH. Copied root-owned to /etc/udev/rules.d/ by
# scripts/setup-dock-lid.sh (never symlinked -- root's udevd reads it). Editing
# this copy changes nothing until `dock-lid-setup` is re-run, and
# `dock-lid-setup --check` reports the difference as drift.
# scripts/test-dock-lid.sh asserts the rule line exactly: udevadm verify
# accepts a rule that never fires (a missing TAG+="systemd", a wrong unit name).
ACTION=="add", SUBSYSTEM=="typec", ENV{DEVTYPE}=="typec_partner", TAG+="systemd", ENV{SYSTEMD_WANTS}+="dock-lid-holdoff@%k-$env{SEQNUM}.service"
